Hebrew:
- be- (in, at), le- (to), mi- (from) are written joined to the noun;
- im (with), bli (without), bishvil (for), al (about) are separate words.

Each preposition fixes the case:
- dative after aus, bei, mit, nach, von, zu;
- accusative after für, ohne.

notes.text:
- at my mother’s is bei meiner Mutter, to my mother is zu meiner Mutter;
- at home – zu Hause, home – nach Hause.

German merges preposition and article: im, am, zum, zur.

Where you would use one locative, one directional and one source suffix, German chooses a preposition by the kind of place:
- in der Schule / am Bahnhof / beim Arzt (where);
- zur Schule / nach Berlin (where to);
- aus Athen / von der Arbeit (from).

If one word for “at” and one verb for “go” cover every place, note that German:
- chooses the preposition by the kind of place and by direction (in der Schule, zur Schule, beim Arzt, nach Berlin);
- puts the article in the matching case.

If one preposition such as “in” or “to” covers many places, German splits by the kind of place:
- in der Schule (inside);
- am Bahnhof (edge, point);
- beim Arzt (at a person’s);
- zu Hause.

For movement: zur Schule, nach Berlin, in die Stadt.
